// ALG1 Unit 06 Lesson 04 Guided Practice
// Lesson: Interpreting Data Displays
// MLA Standards: MLA.A1.DF.3; MLA.A1.DF.4
::ALG1_U06_L04_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.4
Question:
Which display is best for comparing how many students chose each favorite fruit?
{
~A. Box plot#A box plot summarizes numerical data, not category counts.
=B. Bar graph#Correct. A bar graph compares counts across categories.
~C. Histogram#A histogram groups numerical data into intervals.
~D. Scatterplot#A scatterplot compares two numerical variables.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3
Question:
Which display is best for showing the distribution of quiz scores?
{
~A. Pie chart#A pie chart shows parts of a whole, not numerical distribution well.
~B. Bar graph of names#Names are categories and do not show score distribution.
~C. Line graph#A line graph is best for change over time.
=D. Dot plot#Correct. A dot plot shows the distribution of numerical scores.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3
Question:
Which data set is numerical?
{
=A. Minutes spent reading each night#Correct. Minutes are measured with numbers.
~B. Favorite sport#This is categorical because responses are labels.
~C. Type of phone#This is categorical because responses are groups.
~D. Eye color#This is categorical because responses are labels.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.4
Question:
A bar graph shows 14 students chose art and 9 chose music. How many more chose art?
{
~A. 23#This adds the counts instead of comparing them.
~B. 9#This is the music count, not the difference.
=C. 5#Correct. 14 - 9 = 5 more students chose art.
~D. 14#This is the art count, not the difference.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3
Question:
What should you check before interpreting values from any graph?
{
~A. The color of the bars#Color may help visually, but it does not determine the values.
=B. The title, labels, scale, and units#Correct. These features tell what the graph measures and how to read values.
~C. Whether the graph is printed on paper#The format does not determine the data meaning.
~D. Whether the graph has exactly four categories#Graphs can have many valid numbers of categories.
}

